  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how different hydrogel formats are designed and delivered for biomedical use. On the left, three macroscopic hydrogel strategies are shown: an in situ-gelling system formed by a chemical reaction after administration, a macroporous gel that can be compressed and recover its structure, and a shear-thinning gel whose network loosens under force so it can flow and then reform. These examples emphasize injectability, deformability, and adaptable mechanics.\n\nThe center of the image places these materials in the body and highlights possible administration routes. A syringe suggests direct injection, while labels indicate localized uses such as an epicardial implant on the heart and a transdermal patch on the skin. Together, this implies hydrogels can be applied either locally at tissues or introduced in minimally invasive ways.\n\nOn the right, much smaller particulate versions are contrasted by size and route. Microgels, around 10 micrometers, are shown as suitable for pulmonary and oral delivery. Nanogels, much smaller at roughly 10–100 nanometers, are associated with systemic circulation. Overall, the figure conveys that hydrogel form, size, and mechanical behavior are tuned to match different delivery routes and medical applications.",
